g discussion of the univocity of Being and its relation to language and events within the philosophical work", "TWENTY-SIXTH SERIES OF LANGUAGE:A chapter heading in the text, part of the Twenty-Sixth Series of Language, continuing the discussion on language, denotation, and the role of events in language", "MELANIE KLEIN:Melanie Klein is a prominent psychoanalyst and the central figure in Kleinian theory of object relations. Her work centers on the infant’s early mental life and the development of internal object relations that shape personality and psychopathology. Klein developed influential theories of child development, emphasizing the rapid emergence and modulation of internal objects through processes of introjection and projection, and the ongoing dynamics between good and bad objects within the infant’s inner world. She articulated the key early positions—the paranoid-schizoid position and the depressive position—as foundational to later psychic organization, including the emergence of related imagery and affect.\n\nA hallmark of Klein’s theory is the emphasis on the infant’s early mother-infant dyad and the developmental trajectory of object relations. She explored how partial objects and the ongoing tension between love and aggression are managed within the self, and how the “theater of terror” within the infant’s psyche gives rise to the development of internal objects and the subsequent capacity for reparation. Reparation, in Klein’s view, is constitutive of a restorative psychic surface, reflecting the ongoing need to repair fractures in object relations after paranoid and depressive anxieties.\n\nKlein also linked early internal object dynamics to later developments in sexuality and the Oedipus complex, arguing that the schizoid and depressive positions supply foundational elements of this complex and influence the emergence of genital organization and related psychoanalytic imagery. She described the good object, partial objects, and the mechanisms of introjection and projection as central to the way the child internalizes external reality and negotiates relationships with others.\n\nIn sum, Melanie Klein’s Kleinian theory foregrounds the infant’s early mental life, the centrality of object relations, and the enduring influence of paranoid-schizoid and depressive positions on personality, sexuality, and the Oedipus complex. Her work on the internal world, the dynamics of good and bad objects, and the reparative processes that restore psychic harmony has deeply shaped psychoanalytic theory and the understanding of child development and psychopathology.", "LORD ARTHUR SAVILE:Fictional literary character cited by Lacan in discussing regression; from Oscar Wilde's story Lord Arthur Savile's Curse)<|COMPLETE|>", "DOSTOEVSKY:Dostoevsky, a renowned 19th-century Russian novelist, is referenced in the passage as the author whose characters are used to illustrate the discussion of irony, language, and morality.)(\"entity\"", "EPICURUS:EPICURUS is an Ancient Greek philosopher and the founder of Epicureanism and the Epicurean school. The referenced text places him among the earliest figures associated with philosophical pluralism, naming him alongside Lucretius as inaugurators of that pluralistic approach. The discussion ties Epicurus to themes of movement, time, and simulacra, and notes his use of the term “eventa” (which he called symptoms) within this framework. Additionally, the text discusses concepts linked to his thought, such as the swerve (clinamen) and the gradation of time, highlighting Epicurus’s role in early discussions of atomic movement and the nature of time.", "EPICUREANS:An ancient philosophical school that affirms causality and the unity of causes, engages with atomist theory, and argues against destiny being equivalent to necessity; emphasizes the clinamen as a source of contingency in the causal chain", "DAVID HUME:Scottish philosopher; cited in the text as recalling that the origin of belief in gods lies in whim and variability of passions, used to contextualize discussions of religious belief.", "ROBINSON:Robinson is a shipwrecked narrator and the consciousness of the island Speranza; the text presents him as the island's consciousness, whose presence and transformation drive the exploration of space, time, and the Other.", "SPERANZA:The desert island named Speranza; the setting of the narrative where Robinson's consciousness exists and with which he is identified as one and the same, forming the core of the philosophical exploration.", "EMILE ZOLA:French novelist and leading figure in naturalism; prolific 19th-century writer known for social realism and engagement with contemporary issues", "BRUNO (CARROLL):Bruno (Carroll) is a person listed in the index with page references 10, 11, 23, 26-", "ZOLA:Émile Zola, the author associated with Therese Raquin, referenced as (Zola) in the index", "RENE DESCARTES:French philosopher and mathematician; central figure of rationalism; author of Principes de la philosophie; his ideas are cited by Spinoza in the text", "BLYENBERGH:BLYENBERGH is identified as the recipient of Baruch Spinoza’s correspondence, specifically in relation to the theme of the judgment of God. The descriptions indicate that Blyenbergh is mentioned as the addressee (the recipient) of a Spinoza letter, within the collection known as Lettres XIX. In other words, Blyenbergh participates as an interlocutor in Spinoza’s epistolary exchange about divine judgment, with the relevant letter being part of Lettres XIX. The two descriptions corroborate this role, presenting Blyenbergh consistently as the addressee/recipient of Spinoza’s discourse on the judgment of God."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_47b501dd3f23a777a937b8abaeb24878ae74b0580f67ec907afa6dc6023618b6_v2"}